# The data centre of "Steering traffic and cloud circuits": one AS, four
# routers, two circuits to one cloud.
#
#   office 10.1.0.10 -- dcnet -- access --- core --- dc1 ==dx1 169.254.30.0/30== cloud (AS 64600) -- vpc -- app 10.100.0.10
#                                            |                              //
#                                            +------ dc2 ==dx2 169.254.20.0/30
#
# access, core, dc1 and dc2 are the company's AS 65001, joined by iBGP over
# the cables access-core, core-dc1 and core-dc2 only, with static routes
# for the inside cables (where a bigger network would run OSPF). access
# holds the data centre's four networks, 10.1.0.0/24 to 10.1.3.0/24; dc1
# and dc2 each hold one circuit to the cloud and announce the data centre
# as one summary. The cloud's router is scenery, configured and saved, and
# follows a provider's documented rules (the lab's prompt quotes them); the
# company's four routers are the learner's. dx2's addresses, and dc2's
# router ID, are the lower ones, so when everything else about two paths is
# equal, the tie goes to dx2. Each router's interfaces are named after the
# machine or network at the far end. Addresses come from the private ranges
# and the link-local block for circuits (169.254.0.0/16), as the clouds use,
# and AS numbers from the private range (RFC 6996).
machine office
machine app
machine access router
machine core router
machine dc1 router
machine dc2 router
machine cloud router

lan dcnet office:eth0=10.1.0.10/24 access:lan=10.1.0.1/24,10.1.1.1/24,10.1.2.1/24,10.1.3.1/24
link access:core=10.0.1.2/30 core:access=10.0.1.1/30
link core:dc1=10.0.2.1/30 dc1:core=10.0.2.2/30
link core:dc2=10.0.3.1/30 dc2:core=10.0.3.2/30
link dc1:dx1=169.254.30.1/30 cloud:dx1=169.254.30.2/30
link dc2:dx2=169.254.20.1/30 cloud:dx2=169.254.20.2/30
lan vpc cloud:vpc=10.100.0.1/16 app:eth0=10.100.0.10/16

route office default via 10.1.0.1
route app default via 10.100.0.1

run access frr
run core frr
run dc1 frr
run dc2 frr
run cloud frr
run office web-svc
run app web-svc
